WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(cheerful ukulele music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.950
- [Narrator] America loves
Leinenkugel's Summer Shandy.

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:05.030
It's the perfect summertime drink.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.980
But have you ever stopped to wonder,

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.540
what the heck is a shandy?

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.770
- Shandy is a beer, but
it's a beer mixed with

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:13.410
something a little extra.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.710
- [Narrator] That's Dick Leinenkugel,

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.230
his family's been brewing beer since

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.300
his great-great-grandfather
immigrated from Germany.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.260
So in 2006 when Dick went looking

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.330
for the perfect summer
beer, he started by asking:

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.716
- What do the Germans
drink during the summer?

00:00:26.716 --> 00:00:29.770
Kolsch, Berliner Weisse, Hefeweizens.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:32.120
- [Narrator] And then he
discovered the shandy.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.760
In Germany they were mixing
beer with something extra.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:37.840
A tavern owner named--

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.060
- Franz Kugler.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.970
- [Narrator] Was running out
of beer during a bike race.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.820
So, he mixed it with lemonade,

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.721
and thirsty cyclists rejoiced.

00:00:45.721 --> 00:00:47.627
(bike bell rings)

00:00:47.627 --> 00:00:49.200
(crowd cheers)

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.460
Inspired, Dick then turned
to brewer Jackie Laumann.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.670
- I set out to create that perfect balance

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.510
of lemonade flavor with a great beer.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.593
- Voila, Leinenkugel's Summer Shandy.

00:01:01.049 --> 00:01:03.490
The first batch was so popular,

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.243
Leinenkugel's ran out by the 4th of July.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.910
So, the next time someone
asks you, "What is a shandy?"

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.107
You can trace its heritage,
or you can just tell them,

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:16.050
"It tastes like summer."

